Lab 14: Event-Driven Architectures with Camel
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Build event-driven architectures using Apache Camel
Integrate Camel with message brokers (ActiveMQ and Kafka)
Test event-driven workflows in cloud-native environments
Prerequisites
Java JDK 11+ installed
Maven 3.6+ installed
Docker and Docker Compose (for Kafka setup)
Basic understanding of Apache Camel and messaging systems
Lab Setup
Initialize a Maven Project
Create a new Camel project using Maven:

mvn archetype:generate \
    -DarchetypeGroupId=org.apache.camel.archetypes \
    -DarchetypeArtifactId=camel-archetype-java \
    -DarchetypeVersion=3.20.0
Fill in the required details (groupId, artifactId, version).

Add Dependencies
Edit pom.xml to include ActiveMQ and Kafka dependencies:

<dependencies>
    <!-- Camel Core -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
        <artifactId>camel-core</artifactId>
        <version>3.20.0</version>
    </dependency>
    <!-- ActiveMQ -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
        <artifactId>camel-activemq</artifactId>
        <version>3.20.0</version>
    </dependency>
    <!-- Kafka -->
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.camel</groupId>
        <artifactId>camel-kafka</artifactId>
        <version>3.20.0</version>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
Task 1: Set Up Camel Routes for Event-Driven Processing
Subtask 1.1: Create a Basic Event-Driven Route
Create a new Java class EventDrivenRoute.java:

import org.apache.camel.builder.RouteBuilder;

public class EventDrivenRoute extends RouteBuilder {
    @Override
    public void configure() throws Exception {
        from("timer:eventTimer?period=5000")
            .setBody().simple("Event generated at ${date:now:yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss}")
            .log("Event: ${body}");
    }
}
Expected Outcome:
The route generates an event every 5 seconds and logs it.

Subtask 1.2: Add a File-Based Event Trigger
Modify the route to trigger on file creation:

from("file:input?noop=true")
    .log("File event detected: ${header.CamelFileName}");
Test the Route:
Create a file in the input directory.
Verify the log output.
Task 2: Integrate Camel with ActiveMQ
Subtask 2.1: Set Up ActiveMQ
Run ActiveMQ broker using Docker:

docker run -p 61616:61616 -p 8161:8161 rmohr/activemq
Configure ActiveMQ in Camel: Add to EventDrivenRoute.java:

from("activemq:queue:testQueue")
    .log("Received from ActiveMQ: ${body}");
Send a Test Message:

from("timer:activeMqTimer?period=10000")
    .setBody().simple("Test message at ${date:now:HH:mm:ss}")
    .to("activemq:queue:testQueue");
Expected Outcome:
Messages appear in the ActiveMQ queue and are logged every 10 seconds.

Task 3: Integrate Camel with Kafka
Subtask 3.1: Set Up Kafka
Start Kafka using Docker Compose (docker-compose.yml):

version: '3'
services:
  zookeeper:
    image: confluentinc/cp-zookeeper:7.0.0
    ports: ["2181:2181"]
  kafka:
    image: confluentinc/cp-kafka:7.0.0
    ports: ["9092:9092"]
    depends_on: [zookeeper]
Run:

docker-compose up -d
Subtask 3.2: Create Kafka Producer/Consumer Routes
Add to EventDrivenRoute.java:

// Producer
from("timer:kafkaTimer?period=8000")
    .setBody().simple("Kafka message at ${date:now:HH:mm:ss}")
    .to("kafka:testTopic?brokers=localhost:9092");

// Consumer
from("kafka:testTopic?brokers=localhost:9092")
    .log("Received from Kafka: ${body}");
Expected Outcome:
Messages are produced to Kafka every 8 seconds and consumed by the route.
Task 4: Test in Cloud-Native Environments
Subtask 4.1: Deploy to OpenShift
Install the Camel K Operator in OpenShift.
Deploy the route using Camel K:
kamel run EventDrivenRoute.java --dev
Subtask 4.2: Verify Logs
Check pod logs in OpenShift to confirm event processing.

Troubleshooting Tips
ActiveMQ Connection Issues: Verify the broker URL and port.
Kafka Errors: Ensure ZooKeeper and Kafka are running.
OpenShift Deployment: Check resource limits and Camel K operator logs.
